```csharp
public static void ShuffleArray(int[] nums)
{
    Random rand = new Random();
    int n = nums.Length;

    // Move all odd numbers to the front
    int oddIndex = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        if (nums[i] % 2 != 0)
        {
            Swap(nums, i, oddIndex);
            oddIndex++;
        }
    }

    // Shuffle the odd numbers
    for (int i = 0; i < oddIndex - 1; i++)
    {
        int j = rand.Next(i, oddIndex);
        Swap(nums, i, j);
    }

    // Shuffle the even numbers
    for (int i = oddIndex; i < n - 1; i++)
    {
        int j = rand.Next(i, n);
        Swap(nums, i, j);
    }
}

private static void Swap(int[] nums, int i, int j)
{
    int temp = nums[i];
    nums[i] = nums[j];
    nums[j] = temp;
}
```

Explanation:
1. We first move all the odd numbers to the front of the array using the `oddIndex` variable. This ensures that all odd numbers will be before even numbers in the shuffled array.
2. Next, we use a random number generator to shuffle the odd numbers. We iterate from the beginning of the array up to `oddIndex - 1` and for each element, we randomly choose another element in the range `[i, oddIndex)` and swap them.
3. Finally, we shuffle the even numbers in a similar way. We iterate from `oddIndex` up to `n - 1` (where `n` is the length of the array) and for each element, we randomly choose another element in the range `[i, n)` and swap them.

This algorithm has a time complexity of O(n) because we iterate over the array only once for each phase of the shuffling process. It also has a space complexity of O(1) because it doesn't use any additional arrays or data structures. The randomness is achieved by using the input array and a random number generator.

