Next, it checks for an optional leading sign (+/-) and sets a boolean variable `isNegative` accordingly. If the sign is present, it also updates the `startIndex` to skip the sign character.

After that, it checks if the input string is in hexadecimal format by looking for the prefix "0x" or "0X". If it is, it converts the remaining characters after the prefix to their equivalent decimal value. It uses a loop to iterate through each character and multiplies the current value by 16 before adding the decimal equivalent of the current character. It also handles both lowercase and uppercase letters A-F.

