public static void ShuffleArray(int[] array)
{
    Random random = new Random();

    // Iterate through the array from the last element to the first
    for (int i = array.Length - 1; i > 0; i--)
    {
        // Generate a random index between 0 and i (inclusive)
        int randomIndex = random.Next(i + 1);

        // Swap the current element with the element at the random index
        int temp = array[i];
        array[i] = array[randomIndex];
        array[randomIndex] = temp;
    }
}
```

Explanation:

1. We create a `Random` object to generate random numbers.
2. We iterate through the array starting from the last element (`i = array.Length - 1`) and going up to the second element (`i > 0`).
3. In each iteration, we generate a random index between 0 and `i` (inclusive) using `random.Next(i + 1)`.
4. We swap the current element at index `i` with the element at the random index.
5. After the loop, the array will be shuffled, and each element will be moved to a different index than its original position.

Note: The `Random` object should ideally be created outside the method to avoid generating the same sequence of random numbers if the method is called multiple times in quick succession.

