```csharp
using System;

class Program
{
    static bool IsPrime(int number)
    {
        if (number < 2)
            return false;

        for (int i = 2; i <= Math.Sqrt(number); i++)
        {
            if (number % i == 0)
                return false;
        }

        return true;
    }

    static bool IsNarcissisticNumber(int number)
    {
        int digitCount = number.ToString().Length;

        if (!IsPrime(digitCount))
            return false;

        int sum = 0;
        int temp = number;

        while (temp > 0)
        {
            int digit = temp % 10;
            sum += (int)Math.Pow(digit, digitCount);
            temp /= 10;
        }

        return sum == number;
    }

    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        Console.Write("Enter a number: ");
        int number = int.Parse(Console.ReadLine());

        bool isNarcissistic = IsNarcissisticNumber(number);
        Console.WriteLine("Is Narcissistic: " + isNarcissistic);

        Console.ReadKey();
    }
}
```

In this code, the `IsPrime` function checks if a given number is prime. The `IsNarcissisticNumber` function checks if the number of digits of the given number is prime. If not, it immediately returns `false`. Otherwise, it calculates the sum of each digit raised to the power of the number of digits. Finally, it checks if the calculated sum is equal to the original number.

Here's a PascalABC.NET function that generates all combinations of length n of a given array, handling arrays containing duplicate elements and returning the combinations in lexicographical order:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class Program
{
    public static List<List<T>> GetCombinations<T>(T[] array, int n)
    {
        List<List<T>> combinations = new List<List<T>>();
        GenerateCombinations(array, n, new List<T>(), combinations);
        return combinations.OrderBy(c => string.Join("", c)).ToList();
    }

    private static void GenerateCombinations<T>(T[] array, int n, List<T> current, List<List<T>> combinations)
    {
        if (current.Count == n)
        {
            combinations.Add(current.ToList());
            return;
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < array.Length; i++)
        {
            current.Add(array[i]);
            GenerateCombinations(array, n, current, combinations);
            current.RemoveAt(current.Count - 1);
        }
    }

    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        int[] array = { 1, 2, 2, 3 };
        int n = 2;
        List<List<int>> combinations = GetCombinations(array, n);

        foreach (var combination in combinations)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(string.Join(", ", combination));
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, the `GetCombinations` function takes an array `array` and an integer `n` as input. It initializes an empty list `combinations` to store the generated combinations. It then calls the `GenerateCombinations` helper function, passing the array, n, an empty list `current`, and the `combinations` list.

The `GenerateCombinations` function is a recursive function that generates all combinations of length n. It keeps track of the current combination being built using the `current` list. When the length of the current combination equals n, it adds the current combination to the `combinations` list. Otherwise, it iterates through each element in the array, adds it to the current combination, recursively calls itself, and then removes the last element from the current combination before moving on to the next element in the array.

Once all combinations have been generated, the `GetCombinations` function sorts the `combinations` list in lexicographical order by converting each combination to a string and joining its elements. Finally, it returns the sorted combinations.

In the `Main` function, we test the `GetCombinations` function by passing an array containing duplicate elements and n equal to 2. We then print each combination to the console.

