To achieve a time complexity of O(nlogn), we can use the binary search algorithm. Here's an updated version of the code:

```csharp
public static int LongestIncreasingSubsequence(int[] arr)
{
    int n = arr.Length;
    int[] dp = new int[n];
    int len = 1; // Length of longest increasing subsequence

    dp[0] = arr[0];

    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++)
    {
        if (arr[i] < dp[0])
        {
            // New smallest element found, update dp[0]
            dp[0] = arr[i];
        }
        else if (arr[i] >= dp[len - 1])
        {
            // arr[i] extends the longest increasing subsequence
            dp[len] = arr[i];
            len++;
        }
        else
        {
            // arr[i] is somewhere in between the longest increasing subsequence
            // Find the position to insert arr[i] using binary search
            int pos = BinarySearch(dp, 0, len - 1, arr[i]);
            dp[pos] = arr[i];
        }
    }

    return len;
}

private static int BinarySearch(int[] arr, int left, int right, int target)
{
    while (left <= right)
    {
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

        if (arr[mid] == target)
        {
            return mid;
        }
        else if (arr[mid] < target)
        {
            left = mid + 1;
        }
        else
        {
            right = mid - 1;
        }
    }

    return left;
}
```

In this updated code, we maintain an auxiliary array `dp` which stores the longest increasing subsequence found so far. The `len` variable keeps track of the length of the longest increasing subsequence.

The main idea is to iterate through the input array `arr` and update `dp` accordingly. We use a binary search to efficiently find the correct position to insert each element into `dp`.

The time complexity of this algorithm is O(nlogn) since we are performing a binary search for each element, and the overall number of binary searches is proportional to `n`.

